  "query_preview": "..High pressure largely remains in control over Arizona\nthrough the first part of the coming week. Near steady, seasonable\ntemperatures are expected as a result, along with dry conditions.\nBreezy easterly winds today look to give way to calmer winds for \nMonday and Tuesday.\n\nBy the middle of the week, weak troughing will allow for moisture to \nbe gradually advected northward into part of northern Arizona. \nCooler temperatures aloft will also yield modest lapse-rates in the \nlower levels. As a result, a few isolated high-based showers and \npotentially a thunderstorm will be possible during the afternoon \nhours Tuesday and Wednesday. The greatest chances look to mainly \nbe over the higher terrain, from the Kaibab Plateau and western \nMogollon Rim on Tuesday, to eastern eastern on Wednesday.\n\nAn upper-level low looks to dive southward off the California coast \nlate in the forecast period. As this occurs, moisture from Hurricane \nRachel is potentially pulled northward into the southwest. As a result,\ncooler temperatures and potentially a return of more unsettled \nconditions is possible near the end of the forecast period. However,\nuncertainty remains high with this, thus confidence in any exact \ndetails remains low.",
